package di

import "github.com/Xwudao/loom"

// repositoryModule is the project-owned seam for repository providers.
//
// weld writes this file once and never regenerates it, so the wiring below
// survives every later `weld add`. The generated commonModule (di.go) includes
// this module, so a provider declared here is visible to the server graph and to
// every command graph at once. Loom still prunes every provider a root does not
// depend on, so declaring a repository here costs a command that never asks for
// it nothing.
//
// The module is empty by default, so a project that does not use a database
// compiles with no data import. After `weld add db`, register the repositories
// your services consume
// here rather than editing the regenerated di.go. For example:
//
//	import (
//		"__module__/internal/data"
//	)
//
//	var repositoryModule = loom.Module(
//		loom.As[data.Repository](NewRepository),
//	)
//
// NewRepository, declared in di.go, wraps data.NewRepository and is the explicit
// opt-in for the shipped SQL repository; use data.NewRepository directly if you
// prefer. Register a project repository the same way, with the narrow interface
// your own package declares.
//
// A service should depend on a narrow repository interface (data.Repository or
// one your own package declares) rather than on *pgxpool.Pool: the pool is an
// implementation detail, and an interface keeps the service testable and free of
// the database package.
//
// Declaring a repository turns NewPool on only for the roots that depend on it,
// so an unrelated command still needs no database credential and Loom prunes the
// pool everywhere else. Keep the repositoryModule name unchanged: the generated
// commonModule references it.
var repositoryModule = loom.Module()
